1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,206
Dark spots on old bread.

2
00:00:02,206 --> 00:00:04,263
White spots on mustard leaves.

3
00:00:04,263 --> 00:00:06,120
A mushroom after the rain.

4
00:00:06,520 --> 00:00:07,951
All three are fungi.

5
00:00:08,401 --> 00:00:11,818
Diya forgot a loaf of bread
in the kitchen for a week.

6
00:00:11,818 --> 00:00:13,252
Spots spread over it.

7
00:00:13,252 --> 00:00:14,629
Fuzzy, and growing.

8
00:00:14,979 --> 00:00:18,068
That fuzz is a living
fungus, feeding on her bread.

9
00:00:18,518 --> 00:00:22,617
By the end, you will know what makes
a fungus, and the four classes of

10
00:00:22,617 --> 00:00:24,195
fungi with their examples.

11
00:00:24,845 --> 00:00:26,637
Let's zoom into one spot.

12
00:00:26,987 --> 00:00:29,340
Here is the bread, with spots of mould.

13
00:00:29,840 --> 00:00:30,740
Zoom in.

14
00:00:30,580 --> 00:00:34,443
Each spot is a fuzz of fine
threads, standing on the bread.

15
00:00:34,943 --> 00:00:35,843
Zoom again.

16
00:00:35,812 --> 00:00:37,747
The threads are called hyphae.

17
00:00:37,747 --> 00:00:41,089
Together, the network of
hyphae is called a mycelium.

18
00:00:41,589 --> 00:00:43,105
Zoom into one hypha.

19
00:00:43,105 --> 00:00:44,621
Its wall has chitin.

20
00:00:44,621 --> 00:00:48,609
In some fungi, the hypha is
one long tube full of nuclei.

21
00:00:48,609 --> 00:00:50,013
That is coenocytic.

22
00:00:50,013 --> 00:00:52,414
In others, cross walls divide it.

23
00:00:53,064 --> 00:00:54,530
So what makes a fungus?

24
00:00:54,880 --> 00:00:56,421
Fungi are heterotrophic.

25
00:00:56,421 --> 00:00:58,512
They cannot make their own food.

26
00:00:58,512 --> 00:01:02,786
Except for yeasts, which are single
cells, they are made of hyphae.

27
00:01:03,536 --> 00:01:05,865
Most absorb food from dead matter.

28
00:01:05,865 --> 00:01:07,356
They are saprophytes.

29
00:01:07,356 --> 00:01:09,997
Some feed on living plants and animals.

30
00:01:09,997 --> 00:01:11,388
They are parasites.

31
00:01:12,138 --> 00:01:13,795
Some live in partnership.

32
00:01:13,795 --> 00:01:15,844
With algae, they form lichens.

33
00:01:15,844 --> 00:01:18,873
With the roots of plants,
they form mycorrhiza.

34
00:01:19,623 --> 00:01:21,780
Fungi love warm, humid places.

35
00:01:21,780 --> 00:01:24,528
That is why we keep food in the fridge.

36
00:01:25,278 --> 00:01:27,296
Yeast makes bread and beer.

37
00:01:27,296 --> 00:01:29,568
Penicillium gives us antibiotics.

38
00:01:29,568 --> 00:01:31,798
But Puccinia causes wheat rust.

39
00:01:32,448 --> 00:01:37,182
Fungi are sorted into four classes,
by the mycelium, how the spores form,

40
00:01:37,182 --> 00:01:38,757
and the fruiting body.

41
00:01:39,107 --> 00:01:40,969
Class one, Phycomycetes.

42
00:01:40,969 --> 00:01:46,009
They live in water, on damp decaying
wood, or as parasites on plants.

43
00:01:46,009 --> 00:01:48,388
The mycelium has no cross walls.

44
00:01:48,388 --> 00:01:49,767
It is coenocytic.

45
00:01:50,517 --> 00:01:53,574
Their asexual spores
form inside a sporangium.

46
00:01:53,574 --> 00:01:56,167
Two gametes fuse to form a zygospore.

47
00:01:56,167 --> 00:02:01,427
Examples: Mucor, Rhizopus, the bread
mould, and Albugo, on mustard leaves.

48
00:02:02,177 --> 00:02:05,239
Class two, Ascomycetes, the sac fungi.

49
00:02:05,239 --> 00:02:08,538
The mycelium is branched,
with cross walls.

50
00:02:08,538 --> 00:02:12,922
Asexual spores called conidia
form outside, on conidiophores.

51
00:02:12,922 --> 00:02:16,752
Sexual ascospores form
inside a sac called an ascus.

52
00:02:17,502 --> 00:02:22,350
Examples: Penicillium, yeast,
Aspergillus, Claviceps and Neurospora.

53
00:02:22,350 --> 00:02:25,288
Morels and truffles are edible delicacies.

54
00:02:26,038 --> 00:02:28,203
Class three, Basidiomycetes.

55
00:02:28,203 --> 00:02:32,263
Mushrooms, bracket fungi,
puffballs, rusts and smuts.

56
00:02:32,263 --> 00:02:36,569
Four basidiospores form outside,
on a club shaped basidium.

57
00:02:36,569 --> 00:02:39,765
Examples: Agaricus, Ustilago and Puccinia.

58
00:02:40,515 --> 00:02:44,110
Class four, Deuteromycetes,
the imperfect fungi.

59
00:02:44,110 --> 00:02:47,727
Only their asexual or
vegetative stages are known.

60
00:02:47,727 --> 00:02:49,732
They reproduce by conidia.

61
00:02:49,732 --> 00:02:53,539
Examples: Alternaria,
Colletotrichum and Trichoderma.

62
00:02:54,189 --> 00:02:56,390
Two classes are easy to mix up.

63
00:02:56,390 --> 00:02:57,708
Let's compare them.

64
00:02:58,058 --> 00:03:00,345
Ascomycetes, like Penicillium.

65
00:03:00,345 --> 00:03:03,994
The sexual spores form
inside a sac, the ascus.

66
00:03:03,994 --> 00:03:06,123
Inside is called endogenous.

67
00:03:06,573 --> 00:03:09,650
Basidiomycetes, like
Agaricus, the mushroom.

68
00:03:09,650 --> 00:03:12,488
The spores form outside, on a basidium.

69
00:03:12,488 --> 00:03:14,459
Outside is called exogenous.

70
00:03:15,059 --> 00:03:16,011
Your turn.

71
00:03:16,011 --> 00:03:19,414
Puccinia, the wheat rust,
makes basidiospores.

72
00:03:19,414 --> 00:03:22,236
Inside a sac, or outside on a club?

73
00:03:22,236 --> 00:03:23,637
Pause and decide.

74
00:03:26,837 --> 00:03:28,632
Outside, on a club!

75
00:03:28,632 --> 00:03:31,676
Basidiospores always form on a basidium.

76
00:03:31,676 --> 00:03:33,880
Puccinia is a basidiomycete.

77
00:03:34,530 --> 00:03:35,700
Remember this.

78
00:03:35,700 --> 00:03:37,383
Asco, inside a sac.

79
00:03:37,383 --> 00:03:39,570
Basidio, outside on a club.

80
00:03:40,170 --> 00:03:41,872
Now, a worked example.

81
00:03:41,872 --> 00:03:43,675
Six fungi, four classes.

82
00:03:44,025 --> 00:03:46,300
Rhizopus, the bread mould.

83
00:03:46,300 --> 00:03:47,721
No cross walls.

84
00:03:47,721 --> 00:03:48,877
Phycomycetes.

85
00:03:49,327 --> 00:03:51,577
Albugo, on mustard leaves.

86
00:03:51,577 --> 00:03:53,089
Also Phycomycetes.

87
00:03:53,539 --> 00:03:56,344
Penicillium, with ascospores in sacs.

88
00:03:56,344 --> 00:03:57,365
Ascomycetes.

89
00:03:57,815 --> 00:04:01,668
Agaricus and Ustilago,
with spores on a basidium.

90
00:04:01,668 --> 00:04:02,914
Basidiomycetes.

91
00:04:03,414 --> 00:04:06,453
Trichoderma, with no known sexual stage.

92
00:04:06,453 --> 00:04:07,642
Deuteromycetes.

93
00:04:08,292 --> 00:04:09,757
Now, the exam trap.

94
00:04:10,107 --> 00:04:14,271
Many students think Deuteromycetes
can never reproduce sexually.

95
00:04:14,671 --> 00:04:17,944
But their sexual stage
is simply not known yet.

96
00:04:17,944 --> 00:04:22,542
When it is found, the fungus moves
to Ascomycetes or Basidiomycetes.

97
00:04:23,192 --> 00:04:24,627
Where is this asked?

98
00:04:24,627 --> 00:04:29,233
As statements on hyphae and spores,
or matching each fungus to its class.

99
00:04:29,833 --> 00:04:31,050
Exam level now.

100
00:04:31,050 --> 00:04:32,845
Read the four statements.

101
00:04:32,845 --> 00:04:34,537
Which ones are correct?

102
00:04:34,537 --> 00:04:35,805
Pause and solve.

103
00:04:41,205 --> 00:04:42,496
The answer is D.

104
00:04:42,496 --> 00:04:44,697
Statements one, three and four.

105
00:04:45,197 --> 00:04:46,433
Two is wrong.

106
00:04:46,433 --> 00:04:48,951
Ascospores form inside the ascus.

107
00:04:48,951 --> 00:04:51,939
Conidia are the spores on conidiophores.

108
00:04:52,439 --> 00:04:57,061
Remember, endogenous means inside,
and exogenous means outside.

109
00:04:57,661 --> 00:04:59,120
Here is your recap.

110
00:04:59,120 --> 00:05:00,620
Screenshot this card.

111
00:05:03,820 --> 00:05:06,656
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

112
00:05:07,056 --> 00:05:07,956
One.

113
00:05:07,602 --> 00:05:09,637
What is a coenocytic hypha?

114
00:05:10,537 --> 00:05:11,437
Two.

115
00:05:11,098 --> 00:05:14,299
Why are Deuteromycetes
called imperfect fungi?

116
00:05:15,199 --> 00:05:16,099
Three.

117
00:05:15,840 --> 00:05:18,812
Name one example from each class of fungi.

118
00:05:19,912 --> 00:05:23,324
Next time: the bread mould
spreads to the next slice.

119
00:05:23,324 --> 00:05:24,689
How does it travel?

120
00:05:24,689 --> 00:05:28,395
We find out in modes of nutrition
and reproduction in fungi.
